#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bessel_j_zero

#% text
# `bessel_j_zero(nu, k)` gives the `k`-th positive zero of the Bessel function `J_nu`; `bessel_j_zero(nu, k, x0)` gives the `k`-th zero above `x0`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For a non-integer `k = m - t` (`m = ceil(k)`, `0 < t < 1`) the value is the `m`-th positive zero of `J_nu cos(pi t) - Y_nu sin(pi t)`, so that the zeros are continuous in `k` (DLMF 10.21).

#% item
# Numerical values: the sign changes are counted in machine arithmetic, the bracket bisected, and the zero refined by Newton's method at the working precision; exact arguments stay unevaluated and `n` gives the values.

#% item
# `nu > -1`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
n(bessel_j_zero(0, 1))
#% output
#= 2.404825557695773

#% cell
bessel_j_zero(0.0, 2)
#% output
#= 5.520078110286311

#% cell
n(bessel_j_zero(0, 1, 40))
#% output
#= 40.05842576462824

#% cell
n(bessel_j_zero(1, 20, 40), 30)
#% output
#= 101.3126618230387301371410563886519

#% cell
n(bessel_j_zero(1/2, [1, 2, 3]))
#% output
#= [3.141592653589793, 6.283185307179586, 9.42477796076938]

#% text
# A zero of the cylinder function of phase `t = 2/3`:

#% cell
x = n(bessel_j_zero(0, 1 - 2/3), 20); bessel_j(0, x)*cos(2*pi/3) - bessel_y(0, x)*sin(2*pi/3)
#% output
#= ball(5.5251817801679666851e-26, 3.27e-25)

